# /etc/logrotate.d/arcpad-keeper
#
# The alert sink is append-only and nothing rotates it. Left alone it grows
# forever; the growth rate is small but it is not zero.
#
# THE NUMBER, AND WHERE IT COMES FROM. A measured executor heartbeat line is
# 211-215 bytes (counted on keeper/graduate.log, 2026-08-09, before the `src=`
# field added ~9 more). At the executor's 15s interval that is 5,760 heartbeats
# a day, so roughly 1.3 MB/day, plus one OK line per pass. That is a
# calculation from a measured line length, not an observation of a running VPS:
# the actual figure on the box is NOT MEASURED and should be read off `du` after
# a week.
#
# ============ SIZE-BASED, NOT DAILY, AND THAT IS THE DRILL'S DOING ============
#
# docs/runbooks/graduation-window.md section 8: the weekly drill reads the sink
# for evidence inside a window, and rejects a KEEPER_DRILL_SINCE more than 48h
# old. A `daily` rotation that landed between the Safe's proposal and the CI
# job would hand the drill a fresh, nearly empty file and it would report
# "there was no watcher" for a watcher that was alive and had paged. Rotating
# on SIZE makes that collision rare rather than scheduled: at ~1.3 MB/day, 100M
# is on the order of two months of history in the current file.
#
# NO `copytruncate` AND NO POST-ROTATE SIGNAL ARE NEEDED, and that is a
# property of the code rather than luck: keeper/src/alert.ts writes with
# `appendFileSync(path, ...)`, which opens and closes the path on EVERY event.
# It therefore follows a rename on its next write with no held file descriptor
# to invalidate. The forwarder tolerates the other side of it too -- it detects
# a file that shrank, re-reads from the start, and drops records older than
# KEEPER_NOTIFY_MAX_AGE_MS so a rotation cannot become a page storm.

/var/lib/arcpad/alerts.log
/var/lib/arcpad/graduate.log {
    size 100M
    rotate 4
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    delaycompress
    create 0640 arcpad arcpad
}
